title: Atomic Structure & Periodic Table
subject: Chemistry
level: GCSE
emoji: ⚛️
description: Protons, neutrons, electrons, isotopes and the periodic table.
---
What is the relative charge and mass of a proton? :: Charge +1, mass 1.
What is the relative charge and mass of a neutron? :: Charge 0, mass 1.
What is the relative charge and mass of an electron? :: Charge −1, mass very small (≈ 1/2000).
Where are protons and neutrons found? :: In the nucleus.
What is the atomic number? :: The number of protons in an atom (also the number of electrons in a neutral atom).
What is the mass number? :: The total number of protons plus neutrons.
How do you work out the number of neutrons? :: Mass number − atomic number.
What are isotopes? :: Atoms of the same element with the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons.
What is the electron configuration of sodium (Z = 11)? :: 2, 8, 1
What is the electron configuration of chlorine (Z = 17)? :: 2, 8, 7
How many electrons fit in the first three shells? :: 2, 8 and 8.
Who first proposed the 'plum pudding' model? :: J. J. Thomson.
What did Rutherford's gold foil experiment show? :: Most of an atom is empty space with a tiny, dense, positive nucleus.
Who suggested electrons orbit in shells? :: Niels Bohr.
Who discovered the neutron? :: James Chadwick.
How are elements arranged in the modern periodic table? :: In order of increasing atomic number, with similar properties in the same group.
What do elements in the same group have in common? :: The same number of outer-shell electrons, so similar chemical properties.
What are Group 1 elements called? :: The alkali metals.
How does reactivity change down Group 1? :: It increases – the outer electron is lost more easily.
What are Group 7 elements called? :: The halogens.
How does reactivity change down Group 7? :: It decreases – it's harder to gain an electron.
What are Group 0 elements and why are they unreactive? :: The noble gases – they have full outer shells.
